SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/13/pr22195-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-thailand","claim":"The IMF said Thailand should phase out broad-based energy subsidies, target remaining support to vulnerable groups, and shift medium-term spending toward productivity-enhancing investments in education, ICT, green-resilient infrastructure, and expanded social protection, while accelerating private-debt restructuring.","locator":"paragraphs beginning 'The gradual tapering...' through 'In parallel, upgrading physical and ICT infrastructure...' (lines 223-239 on the IMF page)","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF mission statement with specific policy recommendations, contemporaneous to the target year, and directly tied to Thailand's authorities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 contemporaneous policy guidance for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Prayut's government as the incumbent executive authority, though the IMF is recommending policy rather than proving implementation by him alone.","contrary_evidence":["The source is advisory, not a proof of adoption or effective execution.","It shows what should be done, not complete success on the ground."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/09/01/pr22299-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-thailand","claim":"The IMF said Thailand's recovery was fragile and uneven, inflation had accelerated to 5.9 percent in early 2022, and directors welcomed the gradual replacement of untargeted energy subsidies with targeted support, household debt restructuring, fiscal consolidation, and better fiscal governance and transparency.","locator":"paragraphs 240-250 on the IMF page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF Executive Board statement summarizing the 2022 consultation, with concrete macro and policy judgments and direct time fit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 macroeconomic and fiscal assessment within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Prayut's government because it evaluates the authorities' policy response during his premiership, though some levers also sat with the Bank of Thailand.","contrary_evidence":["Some policy levers, especially monetary policy, were not fully controlled by Prayut personally.","The IMF's summary is positive about the authorities' response but still calls the recovery fragile."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand’s Economy Remains Resilient amid Global Headwinds","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/14/thailand-s-economy-remains-resilient-amid-global-headwinds","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand's fiscal response to COVID-19 significantly mitigated household welfare losses, but poverty was projected to rise to 6.6 percent in 2022 as relief measures phased out amid elevated inflation, making more targeted and cost-effective social assistance necessary.","locator":"paragraph 40 on the press release page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank press release summarizing the country report with a precise household-welfare and poverty assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 contemporaneous assessment of the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Prayut-era government response and its distributional effects, not to Prayut personally alone.","contrary_evidence":["The same source says the fiscal response mitigated harm, so the record is mixed rather than purely negative.","This is a country-level assessment, not a ruler-only causal estimate."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rural Thailand Faces the Largest Poverty Challenges with High Income Inequality","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/21/rural-thailand-faces-the-largest-poverty-challenges-with-high-income-inequality","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand's poverty reduction had slowed since 2015, 79 percent of the poor were in rural areas, 70 percent of rural households reported income reductions since March 2020, and policy measures should raise agricultural productivity, improve rural connectivity, and expand digital technology adoption.","locator":"paragraphs 144-150 on the press release page","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank diagnostic with concrete poverty, inequality, and policy findings, directly relevant to 2022 distributional performance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 distributional context and policy advice within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Prayut's government because it describes the economic conditions and policy needs under his administration, though much of the inequality predates 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The source emphasizes inherited structural inequality and pandemic effects, so not all harm is attributable to Prayut.","It identifies policy needs more than executed reforms."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"House passes first reading of THB3tn budget bill","publisher":"The Nation","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/in-focus/40016245","claim":"Thailand's House passed the first reading of a 3.185-trillion-baht draft budget for fiscal 2023, and Prayut said the budget would boost Covid recovery, sustain development, reduce inequality, and distribute aid transparently.","locator":"paragraphs 21-28","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous parliamentary reporting with specific budget figures and the prime minister's own stated purpose for the bill.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 budget-setting evidence within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Prayut as prime minister presenting and defending the budget.","contrary_evidence":["A proposed budget is not the same as successful implementation.","Opposition votes and parliamentary friction show the policy was contested."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand approves tax cut on diesel to ease high prices","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-02-15","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news/thailand-approves-tax-cut-on-diesel-to-ease-high-prices-2764110","claim":"Thailand's cabinet cut the diesel excise tax by 3 baht per litre for three months to help mitigate high energy prices, accepting a revenue reduction of 17 billion baht to hold retail diesel prices under 30 baht per litre.","locator":"Reuters lead paragraph and lines 420-426 on the syndicated page","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous reporting on a specific cabinet decision with concrete fiscal cost and consumer-price effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 shock-management evidence within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Prayut's cabinet and finance ministry decision-making, not to a single-person order alone.","contrary_evidence":["The measure is a subsidy-like response that can be fiscally costly and only temporary.","It addresses prices, not structural productivity."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai economy improving but household debt a problem - Finance Minister","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-26","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/thai-economy-improving-but-household-debt-a-problem--finance-minister-2898611","claim":"Finance Minister Arkhom Termpittayapaisith said Thailand's economy was improving but household debt remained a major problem, and the government would seek new funding and debt restructuring to ease the burden; household debt was about 89 percent of GDP.","locator":"Reuters lead paragraph on the syndicated page","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters interview coverage with concrete debt figures and a stated policy response by the finance minister under Prayut.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence on crisis management and debt conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut-era finance ministry and cabinet response, though debt buildup itself is largely inherited.","contrary_evidence":["The article is about a problem still unresolved in 2022, so it is not pure success evidence.","The debt burden is partly inherited from pre-2022 conditions and the pandemic."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai government spending to target low-income groups - Finance Minister","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/thai-government-spending-to-target-lowincome-groups--finance-minister-2945409","claim":"Finance Minister Arkhom Termpittayapaisith said Thailand's economic stimulus would become more targeted toward vulnerable groups and low-income households as more people returned to work.","locator":"Reuters lead paragraph on the syndicated page","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous reporting on a targeted-fiscal-policy statement by the finance minister, with explicit distributional focus.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy statement within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Prayut-era finance ministry and cabinet, though not a single ruler-only action.","contrary_evidence":["It is a policy intention statement rather than proof of full delivery.","The same article says recovery was still being supported by exports, spending, and tourism."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's annual borrowing and debt management plan for 2023 fiscal year","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-27","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/thai-govt-plans-216-billion-in-new-borrowing-in-2023-fiscal-year-2899579","claim":"Thailand's cabinet approved a debt-management plan that included about 820 billion baht of new borrowing for fiscal 2023, with the plan focusing on infrastructure investment and measures to mitigate oil-price volatility and inflation pressure while keeping public debt within the approved ceiling.","locator":"Reuters syndicated article lead paragraph and summary paragraph on the page","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is strong, but the accessible page is a syndicated copy and the exact lead text is summarized on the snippet rather than directly opened here.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 borrowing and fiscal-planning evidence for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Prayut government through cabinet and finance-ministry approval, not to Prayut alone.","contrary_evidence":["This is a borrowing plan, so it raises debt even while funding stimulus and investment.","The source is less direct than an official budget document."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand: Freedom in the World 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-02-03","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/thailand/freedom-world/2022","claim":"Freedom House said Thailand's anticorruption enforcement was weak, that wide-scale corruption, cronyism, and nepotism had characterized the junta period, and that military sway over government operations left high-level decisions and legislation opaque.","locator":"corruption and transparency sections, including the passages indexed at lines 162 and 168 on the report page","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Established country report by a major rights monitor, directly addressing corruption and government opacity in the Prayut-era system.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The report is published in 2022 and directly describes the target-year regime conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Prayut's governing coalition and military-backed institutional setup, while some corruption patterns predate 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes parliamentary oversight increased openness somewhat, so the picture is mixed.","Some corruption problems were inherited from the junta and earlier institutions."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Corruption Perceptions Index 2022 - Thailand","publisher":"Transparency International","publication_date":"2023-01-31","url":"https://www.transparency.org/en/cpi/2022/index/tha","claim":"Transparency International scored Thailand 36/100 and ranked it 101st of 180 in the 2022 Corruption Perceptions Index, one point better than 2021 but still in the lower half of the global table.","locator":"Thailand country card on the CPI 2022 page","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent global corruption index with a clear Thailand country card and year-specific score/rank.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 index result published just after the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level corruption perception under Prayut's government, not a ruler-personality finding by itself.","contrary_evidence":["CPI is a perception index and does not prove specific acts of corruption by Prayut.","It is broad country context, not a direct governance-action record."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand’s Finance Minister, Mr. Arkhom Termpittayapaisith, has been named Finance Minister of the Year for Asia-Pacific by The Banker","publisher":"Thailand Public Relations Department","publication_date":"2023-01-30","url":"https://thailand.prd.go.th/en/content/page/index/id/154370","claim":"The PRD said Arkhom Termpittayapaisith was honored for economic management during the COVID-19 period, including stimulus measures, support for low-income groups, low-interest loans for tour operators, and measures that helped domestic consumption; the page also states that Thailand was expected to see 2022 GDP growth of 3.3 percent.","locator":"article body and award justification text on the page","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government publicity about a named award, useful as retrospective corroboration of economic stewardship but clearly promotional.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2023 recognition describing 2022-era economic management.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly relevant to Prayut because it concerns his finance minister and cabinet-era management, not Prayut alone.","contrary_evidence":["The page is promotional and retrospective, so it should not be treated as neutral proof of success.","An award does not establish broad household welfare on its own."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary

I screened roughly 30 documents across IMF, World Bank, Reuters, Reuters Institute, Freedom House, Transparency International, the Thai Ministry of Finance, the Thai government PRD, Bangkok Post, Nation Thailand, and Reuters syndication mirrors.

Kept because they added distinct, non-duplicative 2022 claims:
- IMF June 2022 mission statement for policy design, targeted support, debt restructuring, and productivity investment.
- IMF September 2022 Article IV press release for inflation, fiscal consolidation, governance, and household-debt response.
- World Bank December 2022 press release for household-welfare mitigation and poverty trajectory.
- World Bank October 2022 rural diagnostic for distributional concentration, rural income loss, and rural productivity/connectivity needs.
- Nation Thailand / Reuters budget reporting for the 2023 fiscal budget debate in 2022.
- Reuters reporting on diesel tax cuts, low-income targeting, and household debt as a 2022 shock-management package.
- Freedom House and Transparency International for corruption, cronyism, and public-sector integrity.
- PRD award article as retrospective corroboration of the finance minister's 2022-era economic management.

Rejected or deprioritized:
- Reuters Connect image-only records and similar pages with no substantive economic claim.
- Out-of-period 2023-2026 pages that did not add target-year evidence.
- Duplicate Reuters rewrites of the same budget, fuel-price, or debt stories when a cleaner 2022 source already existed.
- General country macro pages that repeated the local structured priors without adding ruler-period attribution.
- Sources that were inaccessible or erroring when a better contemporaneous replacement existed.
- Articles focused on opposition politics or media freedom that were not materially economic, even if they mentioned Prayut.

Remaining gaps

- 5B.2 is still the weakest lens. I have evidence that Arkhom was the finance minister and was later praised for economic management, but I do not have a tight 2022 source showing Prayut deliberately appointed a superior economic team or that he consistently empowered technocratic decision-making over patronage.
- 5B.4 could use a stronger source on actual rule-setting for entrepreneurship, competition, property rights, and investment, beyond the broad budget and recovery statements.
- 5B.7 remains partly inferential. I have IMF and World Bank advice plus policy responses, but less direct evidence of Prayut himself correcting mistakes versus letting technocrats adapt.
- Distributional attribution is better than average but still relies heavily on macro/poverty diagnostics rather than direct ruler-specific delivery data.
